WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.963
Donald Trump didn't invent the imperial presidency. He inherited it.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:06.863
In many ways he's just sort of been the

00:00:06.863 --> 00:00:14.099
very loud, very vocal, extreme energy drink version of what's been on tap all along.

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:21.029
The president exercises, full spectrum dominance, over many areas of American life and law.

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:28.142
This is a person that can launch a catastrophic war, unleash, fire, and fury even start a trade war.

00:00:28.142 --> 00:00:32.953
The president has acquired powers domestically that include

00:00:32.953 --> 00:00:35.566
figuring out what your health insurance is going to cover.

00:00:35.566 --> 00:00:43.870
It's time that we start thinking about reigning in the powers that we've let slip to this institution.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.510
Like a lot of horrible things, the imperial presidency is a bi-partisan creation.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:56.840
You're early in the 20th century, it was the left, the progressives that wanted a dominant and domineering presidency.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:04.839
Later in the 20th century, it's the conservatives who see their political salvation in a strong executive branch.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:08.398
Crisis tends to give you executive power on steroids. 

00:01:08.398 --> 00:01:17.650
World War I, The Great Depression, World War II, The Cold War, 9/11, and The Financial Crisis of 2008.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:23.635
All of these lead to a concentration of power in the executive branch.

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:30.439
Dick Cheney said that he and George W. Bush wanted to leave the presidency stronger than they found it. 

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:36.774
Mission accomplished there, but even presidents who don't go into the presidency 

00:01:36.774 --> 00:01:42.768
with any kind of ideological commitment to expanding executive power, tend to do that.

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:48.177
Congress tends not to resist much in less it's in the hands of a different party.

00:01:48.177 --> 00:01:54.190
There is a ratchet effect when it comes to presidential power, each new executive power grab

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.143
that a new president makes is rarely given back.

00:01:58.143 --> 00:02:03.741
That president's successor will come into the office with a new weapon in his or her arsenal.

00:02:03.741 --> 00:02:10.925
The powers that are forged in one presidency are going to be powers that are handed on to future presidents.

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:17.543
Some of those people are going to be people that you really do not like and do not trust.

00:02:17.543 --> 00:02:24.595
I think it's a mistake to expect any kind of major presidential powers reform coming from the top.

00:02:24.595 --> 00:02:32.505
Nobody who's willing to do what it takes to gain the presidency is going to turn around and say,

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:37.200
you know what now that I'm here, I'd like a lot less power.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.258
If there is to be presidential powers reform and there certainly should be,

00:02:42.258 --> 00:02:44.480
it's going to have to be imposed.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.755
It's not impossible to put the presidency back in its box.

00:02:48.755 --> 00:02:56.379
We need Congress people that act as though they care about the institution itself and the powers that holds.

00:02:56.379 --> 00:02:59.908
And we've seen too little of that in recent decades.

